T1  - Broad impact of adverse childhood experiences on postpartum maternal mental health, child‐rearing behaviors, and child development in Japan
AN  - 2889365062; 2024-16016-001
AB  - The letter presents a study on the broad impact of adverse childhood experiences on postpartum maternal mental health, child‐rearing behaviors, and child development in Japan. Authors investigated the broad impact of both the number and types of adverse childhood experiences (ACEs) using Japanese nation-wide survey data of 5688 postpartum women. Authors largely replicated previous findings on the detrimental effects of different numbers of ACE on maternal mental health, parenting behaviors, and child development. Going beyond previous studies, we found that not all ACEs are equally hurtful. The two factors termed Violence and Family Conflict and Poverty are the primary components associated with these detrimental effects and Violence is the one with most extensive impacts. All three ACE factors are associated with increased risk of postpartum depression and suicidal ideation, which is consistent with neuroimaging findings that exposure to different ACEs produces common alterations in the corticolimbic areas involved in stress coping and emotion regulation. However, physical abuse is also associated with altered hemodynamics in the caudate and putamen, areas involved in reward processing and mother-to-infant bonding. Furthermore, Violence, especially physical abuse and being bullied, carry high injury risks and may cause chronic inflammation that increase the risk of psychopathology and child neurodevelopment problems. This may explain why the odds ratios associated with maternal mental health and child-rearing behaviors for Violence are the largest and that only Violence is associated with MIBD and child neuro-psychological development problems. Meanwhile, parental divorce and witnessing domestic violence have been associated with risky health behaviors including alcohol use and smoking. These findings suggest different ACEs may have detrimental effects via shared as well as diverse mechanisms and call for early interventions to offset the intergenerational transmission of ACEs. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
