TY  - JOUR
T1  - Using psychology for the public good: Making higher education accessible for low-income students
AN  - 2188069041; 2019-04299-015
AB  - The author describes her research and work to make higher education accessible for low-income students. Following the murder of her brother by members of the Ku Klux Klan and American Nazi Party in Greensboro, NC in 1979, the author, then a graduate student, was left wondering if a life as an academic would be enough; she felt she should do "something." Almost 40 years later, she can say without a doubt that she is glad she listened to her advisor, Professor Edmund W. Gordon (tapped by President Lyndon Johnson in 1965, Gordon helped design, and later evaluate, the Head Start program, the most comprehensive early childhood program to date). The author's research has had a direct impact on real people. For example, her research on youth homelessness was cited during debate about a bill that would provide more resources for youths who were “truant.” But perhaps her greatest impact has been through programs and policies she has helped shape through her work in academic administration. She describes the program they developed--the Husky Promise. The program promises low-income Washington-state students, as defined by the requirements for Pell Grants or the Washington State Need Grant (i.e., those with family incomes up to about $60,000 a year), that they can attend the University of Washington tuition-free. Every time she reads about or talks to a student whose life was changed by the Husky Promise, she knows that almost 40 years ago, when she was wavering about whether to continue her studies or take a different course, she made the right choice. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
