TY  - JOUR
T1  - Neuropsychosocial deficits associated with iron deficiency: How long do they last?
AN  - 1528879347; 2013-37988-018
AB  - Comments on the article, 
Functional Significance of Early-Life Iron Deficiency: Outcomes at 25 Years, by Betsy Lozoff et al. (2013). Iron deficiency anemia (IDA) is one of the most important contributing factors to the global burden of disease. One major concern of IDA is how long its neuropsychosocial deficits persist. Lozoff et al. explored the persistence of these deficits. Their reported findings are the next stage of a long-term longitudinal study of individuals who experienced IDA as young children in Costa Rica. Specifically, the authors hypothesized that there would be poorer adult outcomes as the result of a cascade of effects. This approach takes into account the compounding burden of chronic iron deficiency in infancy and its negative impact on brain development, the disadvantaged background some of the subjects grew up in, and the consequences of subsequent poor cognitive, academic, and social-emotional performance associated with IDA. Path analysis was performed exploring the cascade of effects hypothesis. Findings support IDA as a root cause of a number of functional impairments in adults but do not connect the expected additional negative impact of socioeconomic status. It is the current authors view that promoting access to and intake of adequate nutrition by women prior to and during pregnancy is an optimal starting point in the fight against IDA. Further, including iron deficiency as a routine component of clinical decision-making may help promote earlier concern and recognition. With the long-term consequences of IDA more apparent with this new data as reported by Lozoff et al, recognition an imperative step. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
