TY  - JOUR
T1  - Here/in this issue and there/abstract thinking: Human brain development in a dish
AN  - 1442382184; 2013-34851-004
AB  - This issue of the 
Journal draws our attention to the interactions between poverty, nutrition, and the developing brain. Comorboid psychopathology is common in children with autism spectrum disorder (ASD), but little is known about how environmental factors contribute to psychopathology in this population. Diet is well known to affect physical and mental health, but our understanding of this effect is based largely on extreme cases, such as famine, or on short-term effects in adolescents or adults. The brain must contribute to the development of anorexia nervosa (AN), but the resulting starvation also could affect brain function in turn. A recent advance promises a glimpse into the early brain development of living patients. By reprogramming skin biopsies, blood cells, or even tooth pulp, scientists have managed to recreate stem cells from human tissue. The Muotri laboratory reported that induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSC) derived neurons from patients with Rett syndrome are smaller, form fewer synapses, and show signaling deficits compared with controls. Thus far, most human iPSC studies have confirmed or extended our understanding of pathophysiology from cellular or animal models, rather than yielding independent breakthroughs. We can even begin to imagine a day when we test for medication response in a child’s induced neurons before writing the initial prescription. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
