TY  - JOUR
T1  - Review ofFormative experiences: The interaction of caregiving, culture, and developmental psychobiology
AN  - 1033449779; 2012-17445-021
AB  - Reviews the book, 
Formative Experiences: The Interaction of Caregiving, Culture, and Developmental Psychobiology edited by Carol M. Worthman et al. (see record 2010-08663-000). 
Formative Experiences is wide in scope and written from several disciplinary perspectives. The authors include both researchers and practitioners from the fields of neuroscience, developmental psychobiology, anthropology, psychiatry, and psychology. They address the nature of early experience and change at the level of cell activity and brain function, animal research, child development, parent-child interaction, environment and culture, and global events, so the chapters move back and forth between the micro and the macro perspective. A few of the authors work in psychoanalytically informed settings, and some reference psychoanalytic ideas, but most of the chapters come from outside the psychoanalytic field. The book is divided into six sections. The material in each section challenges and expands psychoanalytic thinking in various ways, sometimes questioning its assumptions. Section I introduces cross-cultural concepts of caregiving and development in two chapters that make a strong pitch for the value of a widened empirical look at other cultures to inform an understanding of developmental process. Section II, "How Experience Interacts with Biological Development," examines the ways that nurture affects nature. "Formative Relationships within and across Generations," Section III, asks how early experience shapes an infant's understanding and expression of aggression. Section IV, "Social and Cultural Contexts of Childhood Development: Normative Settings, Practices, and Consequences," grapples with the definitions of normality, health, and "good" child-rearing practices. Section V, "Fear, Fun, and the Boundaries of Social Experience," presents three chapters that address the impact of bullying, social defeat, and the diagnosis of ADHD. Section VI covers the public health, educational, and policy implications of the findings. 
Formative Experiences demonstrates how compatible psychoanalytic thinking is with other disciplines, both neuroscience and the social sciences. Repeatedly, the various authors conclude that nurturing, protective caregiving relationships are central to optimal development, whatever cultural form they take, and that disruptions to this care, whether through parental abuse or neglect, poverty, or global events, distort optimal development. 
Formative Experiences can be read as an implicit challenge to psychoanalysts to articulate the value of psychoanalytic thought to everyday life and to use psychoanalytic knowledge as a force for social change, policy formation, and public health goals that support what psychoanalysts know about how people develop and thrive. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
