TY  - JOUR
T1  - Review ofEarly development risk and disability
AN  - 621153306; 2006-04025-010
AB  - Reviews the book, 
Early development risk and disability by A. Talay-Ongan (Ed.) (2004). 
Early Development Risk and Disability is an updated version of the first edition that was published in 2000. Chapter One contains an overview of theories pertaining to maturation and growth in child development. The author states that the third millennium has become heavily influenced by two frameworks: contextual-cultural transactional theories, and neurobiological models. These frameworks are used to offer insights into: development and intervention in social and relational contexts (Chapter Two); biological roots (Chapter Three); genetics and early brain development, and prenatal development, risk, and the newborn (Chapter Four). These chapters reveal well-defined topics and contain rich and clearly written content. Chapters Five to Eleven cover information on context-related risk factors, language disorders, learning disabilities, pervasive developmental disorders, perceptual impairments, and neurogenic impairment. One chapter refers to gifted and talented children in which issues related to individual characteristics, socioeconomic disadvantage, and underachievement are addressed. The final chapter of the book contains a description of current best practice in early intervention. What I like about the book is the clear intention of the author to move the reader forward from perceiving disability as an individual characteristic to viewing. it as an issue that is related to the child's context and thus requires contextually-driven services. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
