TY  - JOUR
T1  - Foreword
AN  - 620342714; 2004-12349-001
AB  - Substance abuse is a significant public health problem with sequelae ranging from coronary artery disease and cancer, to the transmission of infectious diseases such as HIV and hepatitis C, and addiction. Drug abuse by pregnant women has been shown to have adverse medical consequences on fetal and child development. These adverse medical consequences include but are not limited to: fetal alcohol syndrome, low birth weight, learning disabilities, sudden infant death syndrome, spontaneous abortion, transmission of HIV to the fetus, and medical complications stemming from opiate withdrawal. This journal is devoted to animal models of prenatal drug exposure that can be used to understand the effects of cocaine, methamphetamine, MDMA (ecstasy), nicotine and alcohol, on brain development These models are essential for identifying the effects of drugs of abuse on the fetus during development. Studies in humans cannot accurately determine the effects of prenatal drug effects as these studies are unable to control for a variety of factors that may adversely effect fetal development including: socioeconomic status, educational level, polysubstance abuse, malnutrition, accuracy of self-report, route of administration, dose, duration and time that a substance was used during pregnancy, as well as quality of pre-natal and post-natal care. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
