T1  - Classical music compact discs and books for Georgia's infants: Use and relationship to infant development one year later as reported by low-income caregivers
AN  - 619559161; 2000-95024-013
AB  - Based on the belief that exposure to classical music and book reading would stimulate infants' brain development, in 1998 Georgia's Governor Zell Miller proposed that $105,000 of the state budget be spent on classical music compact discs (CDs) and children's books which would be given to all babies born in Georgia the following year. This program was initiated in July 1998 without any plans for evaluation of either the process or the outcome of the project. The purpose of this study was to assess the impact of Governor Miller's distribution of these CDs and books on caregivers' use of the materials and the effect of exposure to classical music and book reading on infants' communication as perceived by the caregivers. Arnold J. Sameroff's Transactional Model of Development provided the theoretical framework for the study. Participants were low-income caregivers who brought their infants, 11 to 15 months of age, to three primary care clinics. Distribution rates of the CDs and books upon discharge from area hospitals were found to be low. Of 120 caregivers, approximately two-thirds (68%) received the CDs and slightly over half (56%) received the books. The sample for this study was the 81 caregivers who received the CDs; most were mothers (94%), African American (93%), and not married (79%). Data were collected using a Demographic Questionnaire, a researcher developed Music and Books Questionnaire, and two standardized questionnaires, the Infant/Toddler Checklist for Communication and Language Development, and the Ages and Stages Questionnaire. Caregivers reported low use of the CDs and books in the preceding year; however, the books were utilized more than the CDs. Controlling for the infant's age, mother's age, and mother's education, results from hierarchical multiple regressions showed neither infant exposure to classical music CDs and caregiver's interest in music nor use of the children's books and caregiver interest in books were significant predictors of infant communication. Conclusions were that there was poor distribution and minimal use of the CDs and books and that they had no apparent effect on infant communication; however, a longer time period may be needed to assess the impact on overall cognitive development. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
