TY  - JOUR
T1  - Integrating knowledge about biopsychosocial development
AN  - 620493139; 2004-17788-019
AB  - P. K. Trickett suggests that this is an ambitious book (see record 1994-98606-000). That is, the goal is to integrate what is known about the biological, psychological, and social risk factors that threaten optimal human development. As for many edited books, integration is hard to come by, and this one is only partially successful in meeting its goal. The book begins with a historical look at research on developmental risk by Kopp and ends with a concluding chapter by Cicchetti on the central role of developmental psychopathology to the task of integrating these varied risk factors. In between, the book is loosely divided into two sections. The first section includes seven chapters on biological risk factors. There are two chapters on early brain development, prenatal or perinatal damage due to disease or trauma, and plasticity. There are two chapters on genes as risk factors, and two on the role of nutrition and malnutrition on early development. This section ends with a commentary on developmental plasticity and risk. The second section concerns psychological and social risk factors. There are two chapters on poverty and adolescent childbearing, one on poverty and violence, and a commentary. Trickett suggests that the overall impression left after reading this book is how wide and deep the chasm still is between research on biological risk factors and research on psychological and social risk factors. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2017 APA, all rights reserved)
