TY  - JOUR
T1  - Prognostic factors of the intellectual outcome of phenylketonurics: On and off diet
AN  - 616863976; 1984-07239-001
AB  - Evaluated 49 phenylketonurics treated with diet, including 33 whose diets were discontinued, to identify prognostic factors of intellectual outcome. On-diet Ss were aged 11–232 mo; off-diet Ss were aged 60–264 mo. Developmental–intellectual assessments were done annually beginning at 6 mo of age using the Bayley Scales of Infant Development, Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, WISC, and WAIS. While on the diet, negative correlations existed between age at diet initiation, mean serum phenylalanine concentration (SPC), and IQ scores. A positive correlation was found between parental education level and Ss' IQs. IQs were lower if either the initial or the last EEG was abnormal. After discontinuation of diet, longer duration of diet restriction correlated with high IQs. Initial abnormal EEG and higher SPC after discontinuation of diet also correlated with lower IQs. (37 ref) (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
